{"kind":"symbol","primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"languages":["swift"],"tokens":[{"text":"@frozen","kind":"attribute"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"TensorProductView","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"First","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":", ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Second","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":">","kind":"text"}],"platforms":["Linux"]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"type":"heading","text":"Overview","anchor":"overview","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Vertices and their indices are as in "},{"code":"CartesianProductView","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":". The row of (g, h) lists "},{"code":"(g′, h′)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" for"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"each entry g′ of G’s row of g and each entry h′ of H’s row of h, G outer. Rows are computed from"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the factors’ index rows and never hash."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"An edge’s position is ","type":"text"},{"code":"Position(first: e, second: f, reversed:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":": G’s edge ","type":"text"},{"code":"e","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and H’s edge","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"f","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". In an undirected product two non-loop edges {a, b} and {c, d} give two edges, the straight","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"{(a, c), (b, d)} and the reversed {(a, d), (b, c)}, where a, c are the edges’ ","type":"text"},{"code":"u","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ends; when","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"either is a self-loop they coincide and there is one, not reversed. A row lists a factor","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"self-loop from its first appearance only, so every product edge has each of its ends once in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the rows. Directed, ","type":"text"},{"code":"reversed","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is always false, and the product has edge indices","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"index(e)·m_H + index(f)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" when both factors have them; undirected it has none, because a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"self-loop changes how many edges a factor pair gives.","type":"text"}]}]}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/graphproducts\/tensorproductview"]}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts"]]},"sections":[],"abstract":[{"text":"The tensor product G × H of two graphs, both directed or both undirected, as a lazy view. Made","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"by ","type":"text"},{"code":"tensorProductView(_:_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". Also called the direct, categorical or Kronecker product.","type":"text"}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/TensorProductView","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"metadata":{"remoteSource":{"url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/Grafluent\/blob\/main\/Sources\/GraphProducts\/TensorProductView.swift#L41","fileName":"TensorProductView.swift"},"externalID":"s:13GraphProducts17TensorProductViewV","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"TensorProductView","kind":"identifier"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"TensorProductView","kind":"identifier"}],"symbolKind":"struct","modules":[{"name":"GraphProducts"}],"title":"TensorProductView","roleHeading":"Structure","role":"symbol"},"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"major":0,"patch":0},"relationshipsSections":[{"kind":"relationships","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/BidirectionalDirectedGraph","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedGraph","doc:\/\/